The chunked gated delta net: the decay matrix as an outer product, and a shuffle transpose
(lab proposal)

GATED_DELTA_NET is 78 ms of the 536 ms of DSP op time of a 512-token prefill of the 4B, at 1.83
instructions per packet. Qwen3.5 gives the gate the shape [1, H, tokens, seqs], thus g->ne[0] is
1, and with K = 1 and one sequence op_gated_delta_net_chunked accepts the batch: this file is the
kernel that runs, and the sequential thread of gated-delta-net-ops.c is the fallback for K > 1.

Measured on the v79 timing model with tools/htp-lab (target gdn_state), one head and one chunk of
C = 64 at D = 128, the HVX helpers of this kernel cost 86544 cycles together. Two of them are
this change.

1. gdn_ch_decay_matrix cost 15701 cycles. It called two vector exponentials for each of the 64
   rows, 128 in all, to build a matrix that is a rank-1 product:
   E[t][s] = exp(gamma_t - gamma_s) = u[t] * w[s]. The factors need four vector exponentials, and
   a row is then one broadcast and two multiplies. A guard keeps the old path when the log decay
   of the chunk spans more than 80, where a factor would leave the range of an f32.

2. gdn_ch_transpose_f32 cost 24759 cycles, which is 48.4 cycles for each vector of the matrix.
   It issued one Q6_vscatter_RMVwV for each vector, and a scatter of the v79 retires in about 45
   cycles (measured on its own with the target hvxcost). The five-stage shuffle butterfly of
   ssm-conv.c does the same work with five permutes for each vector.

